    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they pretend the future is more predictable than it is. They often present a neat sequence of features, dates, and milestones, creating the impression that product development is a matter of executing a known plan. In reality, products are built in uncertain environments. Customer needs change, competitors move, technical constraints emerge, and ideas that sound convincing in a meeting may fail when tested.\n\nThe first problem is that many roadmaps focus on outputs rather than outcomes. They promise features instead of describing the customer or business problem those features are meant to solve. This encourages teams to measure progress by how much they ship, not by whether the product becomes more useful. A feature can be delivered on time and still have no meaningful impact.\n\nRoadmaps also become political documents. Sales teams want commitments they can share with prospects. Executives want evidence that strategic goals are being addressed. Customers want reassurance that their requests are coming. Product leaders often respond by adding more items and assigning dates before enough is known. The roadmap becomes a collection of promises to different stakeholders rather than a coherent expression of product strategy.\n\nAnother reason roadmaps fail is that they separate planning from learning. Teams may spend months building an item because it appears on the roadmap, even after research suggests that the underlying assumption is wrong. Changing direction can look like poor execution, so people continue with the plan to avoid difficult conversations. The roadmap, which should support decision-making, instead prevents it.\n\nOverloaded roadmaps create a further problem. When everything is a priority, nothing is. Teams divide their attention across too many initiatives, dependencies multiply, and delivery slows. Dates begin to slip, confidence falls, and managers respond by demanding more detailed estimates. This adds administrative effort without reducing uncertainty.\n\nA useful roadmap should not claim to know exactly what will be built far into the future. It should explain which problems matter, why they matter, what outcomes the organization seeks, and what assumptions need to be tested. Near-term work can be specific because the team has more evidence. Longer-term plans should remain flexible.\n\nMost roadmaps do not fail because teams lack discipline. They fail because certainty is rewarded more than honesty. A roadmap succeeds when it helps people make better choices as new information arrives, not when it preserves an outdated plan.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to connect strategy with execution. Yet many become unreliable lists of features, missed deadlines, and outdated assumptions. They fail not because planning is useless, but because organizations often expect roadmaps to provide certainty in an environment defined by uncertainty.\n\nThe first problem is that many roadmaps are not strategic. Instead of expressing customer problems, business outcomes, and important choices, they become inventories of stakeholder requests. Sales wants one feature, executives want another, and competitors inspire several more. The resulting roadmap may satisfy internal politics, but it offers no coherent explanation of how the product will create value.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they are treated as fixed commitments. Teams are asked to predict, months in advance, exactly what they will build and when they will deliver it. But product development involves discovery. Customer needs, technical constraints, market conditions, and regulatory requirements can change quickly. A roadmap that cannot adapt to new evidence forces teams either to build the wrong thing or to appear unreliable when plans change.\n\nAnother common failure is confusing outputs with outcomes. Shipping a dashboard or launching an integration is an output; improving retention, reducing customer effort, or increasing conversion is an outcome. Feature-based roadmaps encourage teams to measure progress by delivery rather than impact. This can create a productive-looking organization that releases frequently without solving meaningful problems.\n\nPoor prioritization makes matters worse. When every request is labeled important, teams become overloaded and fragmented. Frequent changes in direction increase context switching, create technical debt, and prevent initiatives from receiving enough attention to succeed. A roadmap without explicit trade-offs is not a roadmap—it is a wish list.\n\nMany roadmaps also ignore capacity and dependencies. They assume ideal staffing, underestimate maintenance work, and overlook research, testing, infrastructure, security, and coordination with other teams. Dates then become detached from operational reality. Once a few items slip, confidence in the entire plan collapses.\n\nFinally, roadmaps often fail as communication tools. Different audiences interpret them differently: executives see commitments, sales teams see promises, and product teams see hypotheses. Without clear language about confidence, time horizons, and decision criteria, the roadmap creates misalignment rather than clarity.\n\nEffective roadmaps are living strategic documents. They focus on problems and outcomes, distinguish near-term commitments from longer-term possibilities, and make priorities and trade-offs visible. They are reviewed as evidence changes and supported by measurable goals. The best roadmap does not pretend to predict the future. It helps an organization navigate it.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
